NAME
       ed - line editor

SYNOPSIS
       ed [-] [file]

DESCRIPTION
       ed is the line-oriented text editor, a subset of the Seventh
       Edition ed. It needs no screen and works on any terminal.

       ed holds a copy of the file in a buffer in memory; changes
       reach the file only when it is written with w. If a file is
       named, ed reads it into the buffer, prints the number of bytes
       read and remembers the name for later w, r and e commands. If
       the file cannot be read ed prints ?file and starts with an
       empty buffer, still remembering the name. With - ed does not
       print the byte counts of e, r and w, which suits scripts.

       ed reads commands from the standard input, one per line. There
       is no prompt. A command is an optional address or pair of
       addresses followed by a one-letter command and its arguments.
       When ed does not like a command it prints a lone ?; there are
       no longer messages.

       Most commands set the current line, dot, to the last line they
       touch. After a file is read dot is the last line.

ADDRESSES
       n      line number n
       .      the current line
       $      the last line
       /re/   the next line that matches re, searching forward from
              dot and wrapping round past the end
       ?re?   the same, searching backward
       +n -n  n lines after or before dot (or the address before
              them); a bare + or - means 1

       Address parts combine left to right, as in $-3 or /main/+1. An
       empty // or ?? uses the last regular expression.

       Two addresses separated by a comma give a range a,b. In a
       range written a;b dot is set to a before b is read, so 5;+2
       means 5,7. A range a, with no second address runs to $. A
       lone , or % at the start of the command means 1,$.

       A command with no address uses dot (or the default shown
       below). An address with no command prints that line and makes
       it dot; an empty line prints the line after dot.

COMMANDS
       The default addresses are shown in parentheses.

       (.)a   append: read text lines from the input and add them
              after the line. The text ends with a line holding only
              a full stop. 0a adds at the top. Dot becomes the last
              line added.
       (.)i   insert: like a, but before the line.
       (.,.)c change: delete the lines, then read text as for i.
       (.,.)d delete the lines. Dot becomes the line after them, or
              the new last line.
       (.,.)p print the lines.
       (.,.)n print the lines, each preceded by its number and a tab.
       (.,.)s/re/replacement/[g][p]
              substitute: in each line of the range, replace the first
              match of re (with g, every match) by replacement. Any
              character may be used instead of /. In the replacement &
              stands for the matched text, \& for a real &, \\ for a
              backslash and \c for a literal c (\/ for the
              delimiter); so \\& is a backslash and the match. With
              p the last changed line is printed. It is an error if
              no line matches. Dot becomes the last line changed.
       (.,.+1)j
              join the lines into one, with nothing between them.
       (.,.)m addr
              move the lines to after addr (0 for the top).
       (.,.)t addr
              copy (transfer) the lines to after addr.
       (1,$)g/re/command
              global: mark every line in the range that matches re,
              then run command with dot set to each marked line in
              turn. The command is a single line (default p) and may
              not be a, i, c, g or v.
       (1,$)v/re/command
              like g, for the lines that do not match.
       (.)=   print the line number of the addressed line; = alone
              prints the number of lines in the buffer.
       (1,$)w [file]
              write the lines to file (default: the remembered name),
              print the number of bytes written. A file name given
              becomes the remembered name only if there is none yet.
              Writing the whole buffer clears the changed flag;
              writing part of it does not.
       (1,$)W [file]
              like w, but append the lines to the end of file.
       ($)r [file]
              read file (default: the remembered name) into the buffer
              after the line and print the number of bytes read. Dot
              becomes the last line read.
       e [file]
              edit: replace the buffer by file (default: the
              remembered name), which becomes the remembered name.
              On a changed buffer it first prints ?, as q does.
       f [file]
              set the remembered name to file, if given, and print it.
       q      quit. If the buffer was changed since it was last
              written ed prints ? instead; a second q (or e) right
              after it quits (or edits) anyway. End of input is
              treated the same way: on a changed buffer ed prints ?
              and goes on reading; a second end of input quits.
       Q      quit unconditionally.

REGULAR EXPRESSIONS
       c      an ordinary character matches itself
       \c     matches c literally (for . * [ ^ $ \ and the delimiter)
       .      any character
       [...]  any character in the set; a-z is a range
       [^...] any character not in the set
       *      zero or more of the preceding item, as many as possible
       ^      at the start of re: the beginning of the line
       $      at the end of re: the end of the line

EXAMPLES
       ed notes
       ,p                     print the whole buffer
       /main/                 find the next line holding main
       1,$s/colour/color/g    change every colour to color
       g/^#/d                 delete all lines that begin with #
       $a                     add lines at the end ...
       new last line
       .                      ... and stop adding
       w                      write the file back
       q

EXIT STATUS
       0, or 1 if any command failed (printed ?) and the standard
       input is not a terminal, so that a script of ed commands
       fails visibly. Interactive use always exits with 0.

SEE ALSO
       vi(1), grep(1), sh(1)

BUGS
       Lines may be of any length, but patterns are limited to 252
       characters, replacements to 255 and file names to 255.

       There is no u (undo), k (marks), l, x, z, ! or E command, no
       P prompt or H help, and no p, l or n suffix on commands other
       than s. The replacement of s may not contain a newline, so a
       line cannot be split, and \(...\) and \1 are not supported.

       A file that does not end in a newline is read as if it did;
       w always ends the last line with one.
